// wp_pool_mint_step — mint ONE hotframe block and push it onto the free pool.
// THE minting path: every block in the pool, including the one that starts out
// active, is created here and nowhere else.
//
// AXVERITY_EXTENT_WRITE_PATH step 1. The pool depth used to be structural:
// src/mem_controller.m1 minted it by writing the same five-line stanza three
// times, so the depth was the number of times someone had typed it and could
// not be read from configuration. This is the loop_count body that replaced
// that, and the depth is now wp_pool_depth().
//
// Status starts at 0 (not in use), which is what both consumers expect: the
// steady-state acquire in wp_mem_controller_step CASes 0->1 when it claims a
// block, and mem_controller_main's startup acquire does exactly the same to
// the first one. That is why there is no separate "active block" mint — the
// first block is not different, startup was just written separately from the
// steady state.
//
// The state threaded through loop_count is the block CAPACITY, returned
// unchanged on every iteration. loop_count's step fn takes exactly one Int and
// the capacity is the only thing each mint needs, so an invariant state is the
// honest shape here rather than a trick — and it costs no channel round-trip.
//
// This fn must never be hash-consed: it allocates fresh memory and a fresh
// cell per call, so it is declared effect fullIo / deterministic false. A
// merged mint would present as a pool silently one block short, which is the
// same class of trap CLAUDE.md section 6b records for loop_count.
fn wp_pool_mint_step(cap: Int) -> Int {
  let res  = mem_reserve_raw(cap)
  let ptr  = tuple_field(res, Int(0))
  let cell = cell_new_raw(Int(0))
  let _f   = bchan_send(Text("wp-free-pool"), list_of_2(ptr, cell))
  int_add(cap, Int(0))
}
